1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,970
If you're using PLS-SEM for your thesis,
there's a good chance you're making at

2
00:00:04,990 --> 00:00:07,481
least one of these seven mistakes.

3
00:00:07,531 --> 00:00:08,924
And I'm not guessing.

4
00:00:08,974 --> 00:00:13,567
Every point in this video comes straight
from one of the most widely used

5
00:00:13,587 --> 00:00:18,436
references on PLS-SEM: A Primer on Partial
Least Squares Structural Equation

6
00:00:18,456 --> 00:00:23,415
Modeling, third edition, by Hair, Hult,
Ringle, and Sarstedt.

7
00:00:23,465 --> 00:00:25,220
Let's get into it.

8
00:00:25,685 --> 00:00:29,260
Mistake number one: running a simultaneous
F-test.

9
00:00:29,310 --> 00:00:31,889
The F-test belongs to multiple regression.

10
00:00:31,939 --> 00:00:36,158
In chapter six, Hair lays out how to
evaluate a structural model: check

11
00:00:36,178 --> 00:00:40,673
collinearity, test the path coefficients,
assess explanatory power, and assess

12
00:00:40,693 --> 00:00:42,126
predictive power.

13
00:00:42,176 --> 00:00:44,551
There's no F-test on that list.

14
00:00:44,601 --> 00:00:49,050
If you want the combined effect of your
predictors, look at R squared. Hair

15
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:53,093
describes it as the exogenous variables'
combined effects on the endogenous

16
00:00:53,113 --> 00:00:54,117
variable.

17
00:00:54,167 --> 00:00:58,787
So instead of an F-test, report R squared
together with the significance of each

18
00:00:58,807 --> 00:01:00,154
path.

19
00:01:00,691 --> 00:01:04,268
Mistake number two: reporting a
goodness-of-fit index.

20
00:01:04,318 --> 00:01:10,096
The GoF was proposed early on as a way to
validate PLS models globally.

21
00:01:10,146 --> 00:01:14,372
But research showed it can't separate
valid models from invalid ones.

22
00:01:14,422 --> 00:01:19,278
Hair's advice is direct: researchers are
advised not to use this measure in

23
00:01:19,298 --> 00:01:20,413
PLS-SEM.

24
00:01:20,463 --> 00:01:24,533
And the other proposed fit measures? In
their current form, they offer little

25
00:01:24,553 --> 00:01:26,379
value for applied research.

26
00:01:26,429 --> 00:01:31,422
Focus on R squared, effect sizes, and
predictive power instead.

27
00:01:31,917 --> 00:01:37,049
Mistake number three: choosing PLS-SEM
just because your sample is small.

28
00:01:37,099 --> 00:01:42,646
Hair calls this probably the most often
misused argument for using PLS-SEM. No

29
00:01:42,666 --> 00:01:45,416
method has magic capabilities.

30
00:01:45,466 --> 00:01:48,992
The popular ten-times rule is only a rough
guideline.

31
00:01:49,042 --> 00:01:54,303
Think in terms of statistical power
instead. With the inverse square root

32
00:01:54,323 --> 00:01:59,930
method, if your weakest expected path is
about 0.2, you need around 155

33
00:01:59,950 --> 00:02:03,489
observations at a 5% significance level.

34
00:02:03,539 --> 00:02:08,247
And if you ran a pilot study, use its
smallest path coefficient as your starting

35
00:02:08,267 --> 00:02:09,668
point.

36
00:02:10,313 --> 00:02:14,323
Mistake number four: relying on the
Fornell-Larcker criterion.

37
00:02:14,373 --> 00:02:18,284
For decades, it was the go-to test for
discriminant validity.

38
00:02:18,334 --> 00:02:23,152
But Hair notes it performs very poorly
when indicator loadings are similar, which

39
00:02:23,172 --> 00:02:25,813
is usually the case with real data.

40
00:02:25,863 --> 00:02:32,889
Use the HTMT instead: below 0.90, or below
0.85 when your constructs are conceptually

41
00:02:32,909 --> 00:02:34,176
distinct.

42
00:02:34,226 --> 00:02:41,620
In other words, an HTMT above 0.90 is a
red flag for discriminant validity.

43
00:02:42,094 --> 00:02:45,910
Mistake number five: testing mediation
with the Sobel test.

44
00:02:45,960 --> 00:02:50,029
Hair is blunt here: the Sobel test should
no longer be used.

45
00:02:50,079 --> 00:02:55,133
It assumes a normal distribution, which
doesn't fit a nonparametric method like

46
00:02:55,153 --> 00:02:56,181
PLS-SEM.

47
00:02:56,231 --> 00:02:58,739
Bootstrap the indirect effect instead.

48
00:02:58,789 --> 00:03:03,957
Bootstrapping makes no distributional
assumptions, which is exactly why it suits

49
00:03:03,977 --> 00:03:05,510
PLS-SEM.

50
00:03:06,088 --> 00:03:11,002
Mistake number six: celebrating a
reliability of 0.97.

51
00:03:11,052 --> 00:03:16,166
Values between 0.70 and 0.90 are
satisfactory.

52
00:03:16,216 --> 00:03:23,040
But values above 0.90, and definitely
above 0.95, are not desirable.

53
00:03:23,090 --> 00:03:27,187
They usually mean your items are
redundant, slightly rephrasing the very

54
00:03:27,207 --> 00:03:28,510
same question.

55
00:03:28,560 --> 00:03:32,758
So if your reliability looks too perfect,
check whether two items are asking the

56
00:03:32,778 --> 00:03:34,375
same thing.

57
00:03:34,866 --> 00:03:39,250
Mistake number seven: judging R squared
with one universal cutoff.

58
00:03:39,300 --> 00:03:43,667
Hair says acceptable R squared values are
based on the context.

59
00:03:43,717 --> 00:03:50,251
In some disciplines, 0.10 is satisfactory.
In others, researchers expect values above

60
00:03:50,271 --> 00:03:51,450
0.65.

61
00:03:51,500 --> 00:03:56,054
Also remember: the more predictors you
add, the higher R squared tends to get.

62
00:03:56,104 --> 00:04:01,044
So compare your R squared with similar
studies, not with a magic number.

63
00:04:01,585 --> 00:04:02,592
Quick recap.

64
00:04:02,642 --> 00:04:08,402
No F-test. No GoF. No small-sample excuse.
HTMT over Fornell-Larcker. Bootstrapping

65
00:04:08,422 --> 00:04:13,533
over Sobel. Watch out for reliability
that's too high. And read R squared in

66
00:04:13,553 --> 00:04:14,711
context.

67
00:04:14,761 --> 00:04:19,517
If you want all of this in one practical
guide, check out the SEM-PLS guide in the

68
00:04:19,537 --> 00:04:20,823
description.

69
00:04:20,873 --> 00:04:27,264
And subscribe to Not Your Lecture for more
research tips that actually hold up.

